  "prediction": "**Rare Nirvana Photos and Web Preservation Efforts Make Headlines**\n\nIn recent news, rare photographs from Nirvana's first concert in March 1987 have been unearthed and shared by Maggie Poukkula, the 19-year-old daughter of Tony Poukkula, a member of Seattle band Laytem who hosted the fledging outfit's first show in his basement. The photos, which feature Kurt Cobain and Poukkula's father playing together, were discovered tucked away in Poukkula's father's biographies of Cobain and Nirvana.\n\nThe photos come on the heels of a deluge of rare Cobain and Nirvana artifacts, many of which were incorporated into Brett Morgen's acclaimed documentary, Montage of Heck. The documentary, which features unprecedented access to Cobain's archives, is set to return to movie theaters starting August 7th.\n\nMeanwhile, the Internet Archive is working to preserve web pages and fix broken links on the web. The organization is using crawls to archive pages as they are created and archive the pages that they refer to. This way, when referenced pages are changed or taken from the web, a link to the version that was live when the page was written will be preserved. The goal is to make it easier for users to access original content and reduce the number of broken links on the web.\n\nThe Internet Archive's efforts are part of its \"No More 404\" initiative, which aims to preserve web pages and make them accessible even after they are removed from the web. By archiving pages and preserving links, the organization hopes to create a more stable and reliable online environment.\n\nIn related news, Rolling Stone has ranked all 102 Nirvana songs, providing a comprehensive look at the band's complete catalog. The ranking is part of the magazine's ongoing coverage of Nirvana's music and legacy, which continues to inspire and influence new generations of music fans.",
  "answers": [
    "Most teens don't find music history in their dad's stuff, but Maggie Poukkula's dad is Seattle musician Tony Poukkula, who was a friend of Kurt Cobain. Her find? Photos of Nirvana's first concert, performed by then 20-year-old Cobain and his new band in March 1987 in Tony Poukkula's basement. \"My dad showed me them awhile back, but he never mentioned that's what was going on in the photos,\" Maggie Poukkula, 19, tells Rolling Stone. \"I found out because of all the articles. I didn't realize it was such a historical thing. I thought they were just cool pictures of my dad and Kurt jamming together.\" She tweeted the photos last week, and the post quickly went viral. That first concert featured the early Nirvana lineup of Cobain, bassist Krist Novoselic, and drummer Aaron Burckhard playing original songs and Led Zeppelin covers. Similar early-days footage of Cobain and Co. featured in the recent documentary Montage of Heck has renewed interest in the grunge band. The Emmy-nominated project includes home movies, never-before-heard music, and intimate interviews with Cobain's family and friends, notes SPIN. After being critically acclaimed at festivals across the country, the film will return to theaters on Aug. 7, adds Rolling Stone. (Want a little more Cobain history? Here's the mix tape he made at 21.)"
